			<div class="content" id="post-content-4836156">
									

<p>I like space. My condolences on the deletion, kinda stings right.</p>
<p>Can you get a key image of the spooky lights? Could evoke an uncanny effect.</p>
<p>You should try right-click, get-page-source, for 5999, then you have to adapt the default HTML syntax to the module they have, I'm not 100% sure how to do that effectively. But copying an elaborate template, of a 7 off-set creepfest, may not be the right approach. Sound effects will get you further than any other format baroqueness.</p>
<p>DM me your draft.</p>
<p>The idea of something in space taking N years to come kill us is a bit worn. 3200 did it, has upvotes, it worked fine, but the discussion notes the problems, it's kind of mediocre in premise even if it's well-done in execution. The thing about the Bootes void expanding at 1M LY per year, revised down from 100M (which would give it 5-6 years) still didn't work, and a 600 year apocalypse is so far in the future anyway. Scarlet King supposed to arrive in 20 more years, but everyone's too busy scrambling to contain other Apollyons. So I think this would do much better as a mystery.</p>
<p>To continue expounding, I did a Big Rip article that got deleted recently, where Big Rip *may* given present data destroy the universe in 10^38 years or so, any my thing was an observer effect propogated by quasar-faces with memetic properties. Enough memetic contagion, observer effect intensifies, dooming the future to varying degrees up to, Big Rip can happen in a year or less. So this re-draft pitch hits close to home both structurally and personally. It's hard to make looming space doom really work. As a mystery whereas, with minimal Foundation sci-fi colonization, I think it works much better. We can observe but not interact. Maybe they observe us back and this is part of the containment. Anything but a slow moving rock of doom. That it's closer but also lower velocity somewhat helps the issue, but perhaps you can do something more uncanny and persistent.</p>
<p>I really like 179 as a space SCP, not so much because it's Thaumiel, but because it's uncannily humanoid, apparently ancient, and may have something to do with [redacted]. Maybe go for a similar vibe here. More notes after the draft comes my way.</p>

